A||||13||fierofreek2001||all v6 engines the same?||||1||
Z||000000||fierofreek2001||02-02-2001||06:17 PM||||i guess this is a technical question, but are the 85, 86, and 87 v6 engines the same? are they able to be put in different year fieros in other words<BR>thanks<BR>steve||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||02-02-2001||06:20 PM||||Yes they are the same mechanically. Only the 88 V-6's are internally balanced. Cosmetically 85 and 86 have a decal on the upper plenum and 87 it's engraved.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JetroGT||02-02-2001||08:22 PM||||The cross over pipe on the 88's are different as well. It has a heat shield wrapper around it. Some times this causes it to trap moisture and rust it out. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||02-02-2001||10:46 PM||||Must be some smog differences too, since the '85s were rated at 140hp and the rest were rated at 135hp?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-02-2001||11:22 PM||||The Horsepower ratings are different because GM fudged the numbers for emission purposes. If you look at the spec's the 140hp reading was at a higher RPM than the 135 rating. Back then the HP ratings were lowered to ease some spec the EPA was regulating.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||02-03-2001||12:03 AM||||Orief, with the internal balancing of the 88's, would it be a problem to transplant an older year engine into an 88?  I ask because I believe my Formula has an 86 engine in it and cannot seem to get it to run quite right.  It idles somewhat irratically, but is otherwise ok.  Just curious as to how this would affect the engine.  Thanx.<P>------------------<BR>John<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">      <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2m6<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||02-03-2001||12:09 AM||||If vibration is your problem then yes. The flywheel/flexplate are different. I think the balancer on the front of the engine is also different. You need to use the parts with the year of the engine. An unbalaced engine will run rough and possibly idle poorly. It all depends on how "out of balance it is. The balancer on the front could be correct for the engine but the flywheel/flex plate could not and cause minor problems and low RPM.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||mshill||02-03-2001||12:13 AM||||All harmonic balancers are nuetral balanced.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||02-03-2001||04:34 AM||||There were also some improvements to the oiling system in 87-up v6's.||||reg||
Z||000009||2M4 Dale||02-03-2001||08:27 AM||||Correct me if I'm wrong but I believe that the 85-87 V6's used pintle style injectors and in 88 they used a new style called Mul-tech injectors. Also the oil pan changed in 88.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fformula88||02-03-2001||10:23 AM||||Thanks Orief.  When I first test drove the car it was really rough.  So the dealer hads it worked on and they did something to the flywheel.  Since then its been a LOT better, but it still idles rough.  Could the flywheel be correct, but have the wrong flex plate?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||02-03-2001||11:23 AM||||Standard trannys have flywheels and autos have flexplates, ya don't have both.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||02-03-2001||11:39 AM||||Forgive me for being stupid, but whats the difference between a flywheel and flexplate?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||02-03-2001||01:24 PM||||Fly wheel has a surface for the clutch disk to bear against in order to transfer rotational energy to the tranny. Flexplate is in effect a skelatonized flywheel and its purpose is to hold the torque converter. Very simplified explaination. ||||reg||1
